From 066efb64f029ae51b3b4a115c44c5d608537a905 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patch <patch@local>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 20:03:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Consolidate separate pins into one spot on the map

Two people photograph the same cove and their phones disagree by forty
metres, so the chart shows two pins. Zooming in separates them further
rather than resolving them, and nothing in the app could bring them back
together: PlacePicker only ever offered *unplaced* photos, and the
lightbox's re-pin moves one photo at a time.

Merge mode is the missing operation. Tap "Merge" in the top bar, tap the
pins that are really one place, and pick which coordinate survives. It
is only ever a bulk write of one lat/lng onto every photo behind the
selected pins -- there is no merge record, no parent pin, nothing that
needs undoing later. The map draws one pin afterwards because the
coordinates now match, not because anything is grouping them.

  - Destinations are the selected pins themselves (largest first,
    each labelled with any named place and its distance from the
    biggest), their midpoint, or a fresh point tapped on the map. The
    midpoint is one vote per pin rather than per photo, so a pin holding
    forty shots doesn't drag it onto itself.
  - `groupByPin` groups by exact stored coordinate, deliberately the
    opposite of `clusterPhotos`: merging is about the difference between
    two readings 40 m apart, so proximity grouping would hide the very
    thing being fixed.
  - Selection is per marker, all or nothing. A cluster is several pins
    the map happened to draw as one at this zoom, so half-selecting it
    would mean selecting something you can't currently see. The tray
    reports pins and photos separately, and refuses to continue below
    two pins -- merging a pin into itself is a no-op with a confirmation
    dialog attached.
  - MapCanvas paints selection by writing data attributes on the marker
    elements directly. Asking the clusterer to re-render is the obvious
    route and silently does nothing: its render() only redraws when the
    algorithm reports the *clusters* changed, and selecting a pin
    doesn't change any cluster.
  - Naming falls out for free. The existing prompt fires whenever a
    write lands somewhere unnamed, which after a merge is exactly right:
    the reason several pins were one place is usually a name nobody had
    written down.

Two fixes to existing behaviour that this leaned on:

Pins now report their own coordinate in pick mode instead of opening.
Markers sit above the map and swallow the click, so the one spot you
were aiming at was the one place the map didn't respond -- and "put it
exactly where that photo is" is the commonest thing anyone wants when
placing by hand. Clusters answer with their own averaged position.

The 200-id cap on PATCH /api/photos/location is now handled rather than
tripped over. The route 400s the whole request when a selection exceeds
it, so "select all" on a long unplaced list, a large gallery selection,
or a merge of two busy pins moved nothing at all. Chunking lives in the
new writeLocation, shared by both pages, which also collapses the
duplicate request-and-reconcile logic the Browse selection introduced.
Both sheets now treat a total refusal as a resolved `false` rather than
a thrown error -- it's an ordinary outcome of a permissive route, not an
exception.
